The standard approach in .NET is to **wrap file system calls behind an interface** so you can substitute a fake/mock in tests:

```csharp
public interface IFileSystem
{
    string ReadAllText(string path);
    void WriteAllText(string path, string contents);
    bool DirectoryExists(string path);
}

public class PhysicalFileSystem : IFileSystem
{
    public string ReadAllText(string path) => File.ReadAllText(path);
    public void WriteAllText(string path, string contents) => File.WriteAllText(path, contents);
    public bool DirectoryExists(string path) => Directory.Exists(path);
}
```

Then inject `IFileSystem` into classes that need it. In tests, use a mock (e.g., Moq/NSubstitute) or a simple in-memory implementation.

**Alternatively**, use the built-in `System.IO.Abstractions` NuGet package — it provides `IFileSystem` and a `MockFileSystem` out of the box, saving you from hand-rolling wrappers:

```bash
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions
dotnet add package System.IO.Abstractions.TestingHelpers  # for tests
```

```csharp
// Production: inject FileSystem
var fs = new FileSystem();

// Test: inject MockFileSystem
var fs = new MockFileSystem(new Dictionary<string, MockFileData>
{
    { "/app/config.json", new MockFileData("{}") }
});
```

**TL;DR**: Depend on an abstraction (`IFileSystem`), inject it via DI, and swap in a fake for tests. `System.IO.Abstractions` is the most popular off-the-shelf option.
